# EVERY PATTERN ABOVE IS TOP-LEVEL-ONLY, and that is not a style choice: Docker
# matches these against the path relative to the context root, so `tests/`
# excludes the top-level tests directory and nothing nested below it. A stale
# nested checkout here therefore had its test files type-checked by a release
# build, which failed after the version tag was already published.
#
# scripts/deploy.sh no longer builds from this directory — it builds from a
# separate permanent checkout holding the tracked tree at the deployed commit
# and nothing else — so no pattern in this file is what keeps that class out of
# a released image any more. The entry below is for the other caller: a
# `docker compose build` run by hand in this directory, where these patterns
# are still the only thing between the working tree and the image.
worktrees/
